    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1125">

                <pb facs="0940" n="824" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg824"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 612.1123/129.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mobile, Alabama</hi>
                        </placeName>[, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-16">undated</date>] [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">received March 16, 1915</hi>].</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In the conference yesterday the terms “closed port and open port” were used
                    instead of the term “blockade”. Therefore it is understood that in view of
                    General Carranza’s order closing the port his agreement yesterday to annul that
                    order means that Mexican consuls will now be authorized to clear vessels for
                    Progreso. An interesting feature of the discussion, in which Cabrera was the
                    spokesman, was the point made by Carranza himself that in his opinion a request
                    from the British Government to the American Government during our civil war to
                    discontinue its blockade of New Orleans because British manufacturers needed the
                    cotton would be an appropriate comparison.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Silliman</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
